// relation: large-sample-pvalue-is-probability
// holds because: The touched computation is documented to return a two-sided asymptotic p-value / observed significance level; for any valid input, a correct implementation must therefore return a finite probability in [0, 1]. This directly targets the changed n1*n2 product in the asymptotic formula at sizes just beyond 32-bit overflow for int products.
// valid input: Build two non-null, non-empty samples of equal size n chosen with data.consumeInt(46341, 47000), so n*n exceeds Integer.MAX_VALUE. Fill x with strictly smaller values than all values in y (e.g. x[i]=i, y[i]=i+n+1). These are valid inputs and probe the exact overflow boundary governed by the patched product expression.
int n = data.consumeInt(46341, 47000);
double[] x = new double[n];
double[] y = new double[n];
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    x[i] = i;
    y[i] = i + n + 1.0;
}
MannWhitneyUTest t = new MannWhitneyUTest();
double p;
try {
    p = t.mannWhitneyUTest(x, y);
} catch (Exception e) {
    return;
}
if (!(Double.isFinite(p) && p >= 0.0 && p <= 1.0)) {
    throw new RuntimeException("relation large-sample-pvalue-is-probability violated: p=" + p + " for n=" + n);
}

